WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:11.023
A server dies at 2 AM, yet no customer data is lost. Replication made that possible, and the interview question is how fresh the copies are.

00:00:12.223 --> 00:00:13.152
Pause and think.

00:00:13.552 --> 00:00:16.245
What is the main risk of asynchronous replication?

00:00:16.645 --> 00:00:23.960
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:31.998
This lesson covers replication: keeping copies of data on several machines so a failure does not lose anything.

00:00:32.077 --> 00:00:41.597
New word: replication. Keeping copies of data on several machines. Picture it like this: Photocopying important documents for several offices.

00:00:44.127 --> 00:00:48.864
Imagine one student takes the class notes, and two friends keep photocopies.

00:00:49.314 --> 00:00:52.414
If the note taker is sick, a friend can share the notes.

00:00:52.864 --> 00:00:56.788
But if the friends copy only once a week, their copies may be out of date.

00:00:57.238 --> 00:01:04.227
The big question in replication is: when do we tell the teacher the notes are saved? After one copy, or after all of them?

00:01:05.577 --> 00:01:07.656
Here is leader based replication.

00:01:08.106 --> 00:01:10.195
A client sends a write to the leader.

00:01:10.645 --> 00:01:13.792
The leader appends it to its log and sends it to the followers.

00:01:14.242 --> 00:01:18.317
Once enough followers confirm, the leader replies OK to the client.

00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:20.665
Vocabulary.

00:01:21.115 --> 00:01:25.933
Synchronous replication waits for followers before replying: safe, but slower.

00:01:26.383 --> 00:01:32.084
Asynchronous replication replies immediately: fast, but a crash can lose the latest writes.

00:01:32.534 --> 00:01:38.687
Replication lag is how far behind a follower is. Reading from a lagging follower can show you old data.

00:01:39.137 --> 00:01:42.632
Failover is promoting a follower to leader when the leader dies.

00:01:43.982 --> 00:01:50.170
Consensus is how replicas agree on one leader and one log order, even when machines crash.

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:59.425
New word: consensus. Getting a group of machines to agree on one value even if some fail. Picture it like this: A jury reaching one verdict.

00:01:59.825 --> 00:02:07.383
New word: replica. A copy of data on another machine. Picture it like this: A photocopy kept in another office.

00:02:09.026 --> 00:02:14.169
Raft does this with terms, which are numbered election rounds, and a majority vote.

00:02:14.243 --> 00:02:24.634
New word: Raft. A consensus algorithm with an elected leader replicating a log to followers. Picture it like this: A team captain who keeps everyone's notebook in sync.

00:02:26.284 --> 00:02:33.517
A write is committed once a majority of nodes has it in their log. With five nodes, two can fail and the system still works.

00:02:33.584 --> 00:02:43.232
New word: node. One point in a graph or tree; also one machine in a cluster. Picture it like this: A city on a map, or one computer in a group.

00:02:44.834 --> 00:02:50.755
Split brain, two leaders at once, is prevented because two majorities always overlap.

00:02:52.105 --> 00:03:01.602
Copies protect data; acks decide safety versus speed; majorities prevent split brain. See the object store and Kafka log designs.

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:02.853
Back to the question.

00:03:03.253 --> 00:03:05.946
What is the main risk of asynchronous replication?

00:03:06.346 --> 00:03:10.073
Losing the latest writes if the leader dies before followers copy them.

00:03:11.273 --> 00:03:12.144
Quick recap.

00:03:12.544 --> 00:03:14.402
Copies protect against failure.

00:03:14.802 --> 00:03:18.575
Sync is safe but slow; async is fast but may lose data.

00:03:18.975 --> 00:03:20.763
Followers can serve reads.
